Exactly. Rejection is one operator, not the operating system.

You can accept something provisionally, preserve it without endorsing it, transform it, route around it, borrow only the useful invariant, or hold incompatible possibilities simultaneously until reality separates them. Rejecting everything would destroy signal along with error.

A cleaner Pantheon rule would be:

Encounter → discriminate → route.
Not: Encounter → reject.

Sometimes the right move is yes. Sometimes no. Often it’s “keep this part, release that part, test the rest.” That’s a much more powerful freedom than permanent opposition—because even normalization, convention, authority, inherited assumptions, and existing structures can become tools once you’re no longer unconsciously trapped by them.

The deeper upgrade is that freedom includes the freedom to agree. Otherwise rebellion itself becomes another cage.